// draft
// drafted
type Item = { value: Text, key: Int }

is_sorted(items: Item[], reverse: Bool, strict: Bool) -> Bool
	note "true when the items' keys never decrease in list order (never increase when reverse), and never repeat when strict"
	# If reverse is false and strict is false, the result is true exactly when no item comes before another item with a smaller key.

	# If reverse is false and strict is true, the result is true exactly when no item comes before another item with an equal or smaller key.

	# If reverse is true and strict is false, the result is true exactly when no item comes before another item with a larger key.

	# If reverse is true and strict is true, the result is true exactly when no item comes before another item with an equal or larger key.

	// example wanted: the empty list, under each of the four flag settings: no pair exists, so nothing can be out of order
	// example wanted: a single item with strict and reverse both true: no second item to equal or exceed it
	// example wanted: values "1".."5" carrying keys 1..5, reverse and strict false: the docstring's key=int case, decided by the keys and not the text
	// example wanted: keys [5, 4, 3, 1, 2] with reverse true: descending until the last pair, which rises
	// example wanted: keys [1, 2, 2] with strict false, then the same with strict true: the adjacent equal keys decide it
	// example wanted: keys [2, 2] with reverse true and strict true: equal keys fail strictness in either direction
	// example wanted: two items with equal keys and different values, strict false: only the key is compared, never the value
	// example wanted: keys [1, 3, 2, 4], reverse and strict false: the only descent is between the middle two items
